Fail2Ban on FenrirOS
====================

Installed inert: the service is not enabled and no jail is enabled.
/etc/fail2ban holds upstream's defaults (jail.conf defines jails but enables
none; filter.d and action.d are the stock filters and actions). Your changes
belong in /etc/fail2ban/jail.local, jail.d/*.local and fail2ban.local, never
in the stock files.

1. cp /usr/share/doc/fail2ban/examples/jail.local.example /etc/fail2ban/jail.local
   and edit it: enable the jails you want, set logpath (your syslog's file) and
   the banaction your firewall needs (nftables, iptables or ipset must be
   installed; fail2ban only calls them).
2. fail2ban-client -t        (tests the configuration)
3. dinitctl enable fail2ban

Logs go to /var/log/fail2ban.log (fail2ban.conf), the socket to
/run/fail2ban, the ban database to /var/lib/fail2ban. A logrotate example is
in /usr/share/doc/fail2ban/examples. The inotify back end (pyinotify) and
journald (python-systemd) back ends are not available; the default back end
polls the log files.
